## 5. ACCESS CONTROL (the core of the product)

All permission data is editable by Super Admin from UI. Implement exactly:

- **Permission** = `module.action` key from `Permissions_Catalog`. Catalog covers: employee profile, sensitive fields (`field.salary.view/edit`, `field.bank.view/edit`, `field.govtid.view`, `field.medical.view`), org setup, attendance (`markself, markothers, view, correct, regularize.approve, lock, export`), geofence and attendance policy, selfie view, leave, calendar, salary structure and revision, payroll (`prepare, verify, approve, release, reopen`, payslip view, statutory export, F&F), advances/loans/expenses, documents (per category), recruitment, performance, exit, approvals/workflow (`inbox, delegate, override, define`), reports (per report, view/export), users and access (`user.create, user.resetpassword, role.define, role.assign, override.grant, authority.delegate, accesslog.view`), system settings, backup/restore, announcements. Each has an `sensitive` flag.
- **Actions** per module include View, Create, Edit, Delete (soft), Approve, Export plus module-specific ones.
- **Scope:** Self, Direct reports, Team (full downline via reporting line), Department, Branch, Company, Custom list. Resolve scope on the server for every record touched.
- **Role** = permissions + scope + optional condition (e.g. `amount_max`). Super Admin can create, clone, edit, deactivate roles. Multiple roles per user. **Template roles** (HR Manager, Department Head, Supervisor, Staff, Accounts, Executive) are offered as optional "Apply template" buttons in the Role Builder, never auto-seeded.
- **User overrides:** ALLOW or DENY per permission with mandatory reason and expiry.
- **Authority delegation:** Super Admin gives a higher authority (CEO/Director/HR Head) an **Authority profile**: scope, capability ceiling (what they may grant onward), workflow-definition rights, validity dates. Authorities can assign roles/overrides and define workflows **only within their ceiling and scope**. They can never touch Super Admin settings, backup/restore, audit log, or their own permissions, and cannot escalate themselves.
- **Evaluation order (implement exactly):** session valid and user active, then explicit DENY override, then union of roles + active overrides + active delegations, then permission key present, then scope check, then field-level check (mask sensitive fields), then feature-switch check, then maker-checker/segregation of duties, then condition check, then execute and write to `Audit_Logs`. Default deny. Deny beats allow.
- Safeguards: max 2 Super Admins (configurable lower bound 1, upper bound 2); sensitive grants require Super Admin; sensitive field views are logged; leaver rule (deactivation revokes login, roles, reassigns pending approvals); quarterly Access Review report; "Test as user" preview mode for Super Admin that never exposes real passwords and is audited; effective-permissions viewer ("what can this user do?").

